      <title>Government Will Remain Funded Until Early December</title>
      <author>Amy West       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/622/2026-09-04_government-will-remain-funded-until-early-december</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/622/2026-09-04_government-will-remain-funded-until-early-december</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 04 Sep 2026 15:05:18 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;The House was in session for votes this week and they got the one thing they really needed to do done: the government will remain funded until December 11.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;On Thursday September 3, &lt;a href="https://bsky.app/profile/monaburns.bsky.social/post/3mumm43bnjk2v"&gt;House Leadership announced that they will start their “October Recess” on September 15&lt;/a&gt;. This means the House will be out of session from September 15 through at least November 9. The stated reasons, &lt;a href="https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2026/09/03/congress/house-republicans-poised-to-cut-pre-election-session-short-01063447"&gt;according to Politico&lt;/a&gt;, are so GOP (and presumably Dem too) members can campaign and because as usual, Speaker Johnson is having trouble corralling his members on tight votes.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;At the moment, the Senate is still expected to return September 14 - October 2 and then also exit to campaign. This could change.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Government Remains Funded&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr6500"&gt;H.R. 6500: Continuing Appropriations and Extensions Act, 2027&lt;/a&gt; which funds the government through December 11 at current levels passed on September 1 and was signed by the President on September 2nd. It passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h286"&gt;370-48&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.congress.gov/crs-appropriations-status-table"&gt;No regular fiscal year appropriations bills have passed yet&lt;/a&gt; and the new fiscal year starts October 1. If any regular appropriations bills are passed, its unlikely to occur until some time in 2027.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Chuck Edwards Censured&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hres1498"&gt;H.Res. 1498&lt;/a&gt;: In the matter of allegations relating to Representative Chuck Edwards passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h292"&gt;413-2&lt;/a&gt; after Rep. Edwards requested a recorded vote. Rep. Edwards will leave Congress at the end of this term. The House Committee on Ethics concluded  in early August that there was substantial reason to believe &lt;a href="https://ethics.house.gov/press-releases/statement-of-the-chairman-and-ranking-member-of-the-committee-on-ethics-regarding-representative-chuck-edwards-2/"&gt;Representative Edwards violated House Rules related to sexual harassment and hostile workplaces&lt;/a&gt; and recommended the censure vote that took place this week.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Resolution on Constitutional Amendment Regarding Supreme Court Fails&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hjres1"&gt;H.J.Res. 1&lt;/a&gt;: Proposing an amendment to the Constitution of the United States to require that the Supreme Court of the United States be composed of nine justices failed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h293"&gt;212-206&lt;/a&gt;. But it got a majority! Yes, but it came to the floor under suspension of the rules and required a 2/3rds majority to pass. This was an odd legislative maneuver for a few reasons. One, it could have come to the floor like normal and would have passed because then it only needed a simple majority, which it got. Two, the Supreme Court’s size can be changed (or fixed at current levels or reduced) by statute. &lt;a href="https://jamiedupree.substack.com/p/trump-rallies-with-swing-seat-gop"&gt;Jamie Dupree goes into more detail&lt;/a&gt;. Presumably, as Dupree notes, the goal was for it to fail and use that for campaign purposes since there are many other ways to adjust the Supreme Court’s size that would have a much better chance of actually becoming law.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;h2&gt;New Laws&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;These three, plus the continuing resolution means that all four bills that could become law will. These three still need to go to the President for signing to be officially all done.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s858"&gt;S. 858&lt;/a&gt;: Hershel ‘Woody’ Williams National Medal of Honor Monument Location Act passed by voice vote.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s32"&gt;S. 32: LACA&lt;/a&gt;, which clarifies where court may be held for certain district courts in Texas and California, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h285"&gt;378-10&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s307"&gt;S. 307: Prison Staff Safety Enhancement Act&lt;/a&gt;, which addresses sexual harassment and sexual assault of Bureau of Prisons staff in prisons, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h284"&gt;376-8&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Other Votes&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;None of these bills are close to becoming law yet; they still need to pass the Senate in the same form first.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr4795"&gt;H.R. 4795: Protect Economic and Academic Freedom Act of 2026&lt;/a&gt;, which would &lt;a href="https://www.cbo.gov/publication/62739"&gt;prohibit colleges and universities from refusing to interact with Israeli businesses and educational institutions or lose federal funding&lt;/a&gt;, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h295"&gt;237-169&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hres1490"&gt;H.Res. 1490&lt;/a&gt;: Providing for the condemnation and denouncement of socialism in all its forms, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h289"&gt;220-192&lt;/a&gt;. This is a “simple resolution” and it is at the end of its legislative process.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr9436"&gt;H.R. 9436: Northeast Lobsterman Protection Act of 2026&lt;/a&gt;, which would &lt;a href="https://www.nationalfisherman.com/house-to-take-up-bill-extending-right-whale-regulatory-pause-to-2035"&gt;extend the pause on right whale regulations&lt;/a&gt;, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h291"&gt;219-196&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h294"&gt;H.R. 1501: Protecting Domestic Mining Act of 2025&lt;/a&gt;, which would amend the FAST Act to include certain mineral production activities as a covered project (in other words, to speed up permitting for mines), passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h294"&gt;218-201&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;h2&gt;House Rules’ Member Day&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;We will hear more from our colleagues at the First Branch Forecast on this topic soon, but this week the House Rules Committee held its “Member Day”. This is when members can come to the committee to suggest changes to how the House functions in the next Congress since with the start of each new Congress every two years, there’s an opportunity to change the rules under which it operates. &lt;a href="https://www.notus.org/analysis/congress-house-bipartianship"&gt;NOTUS wrote about it&lt;/a&gt; and suggested that training on how to handled classified information is a bipartisan concern and need along with the option to cast votes remotely while dealing with newborns or other family medical care.&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>Legislative Recap</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>House Popping Back In this Week</title>
      <author>Amy West       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/621/2026-08-31_house-popping-back-in-this-week</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/621/2026-08-31_house-popping-back-in-this-week</guid>
      <pubDate>Mon, 31 Aug 2026 14:26:55 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Update, September 2, 2026: We incorrectly identified which member of the House new &lt;a href="https://www.ajc.com/politics/2026/09/everton-blair-georgias-first-openly-gay-congressman-sworn-into-office/"&gt;Rep. Everton Blair (D-GA13)&lt;/a&gt; was replacing. It is David Scott who died earlier this year. Our apologies.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The House started its August(ish) recess earlier than the Senate and so they’re returning slightly earlier. Of course, then they’re leaving again for next week and &lt;a href="https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2026/08/31/congress/johnson-faces-pressure-to-cut-out-early-01056835"&gt;maybe staying gone until November&lt;/a&gt;, but for now they’re in session.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;If the House comes back before November, then both chambers will be in session from Sept. 14 until the beginning of October.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/house-returns-five-weeks-away-top-priority-avoid-government-shutdown-rcna594745"&gt;highest priority this week is passing the continuing resolution the Senate passed&lt;/a&gt; before it began its recess. The continuing resolution is going to the floor under suspension of the rules meaning House leadership expects it to have the 2/3rds majority in favor for passage even if a small number of Republican members vote no. Should it pass, then the government will stay funded at current levels through December 11 and that’s what might trigger the early exit to campaign before the election.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;New House Member&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;There have been primaries and at least one special election during August. In Georgia, in the GA-13 special election to fill the deceased David Scott’s seat through January, Rep-Elect Everton Blair was elected. He is expected to be &lt;a href="https://bsky.app/profile/monaburns.bsky.social/post/3muez5y2mz22s"&gt;sworn in today&lt;/a&gt; and will be the first out gay Black representative from the South.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Four New Laws Possible This Week&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Reviewing &lt;a href="https://www.majorityleader.gov/schedule/weekly-schedule.htm"&gt;the House’s Weekly Schedule page&lt;/a&gt;, there are four bills, which if passed by the House, will then go to the President for signing and even if he doesn’t sign them, will still become law eventually:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s32"&gt;S. 32 LACA&lt;/a&gt;, which would  clarify where court may be held for certain district courts in Texas and California&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s307"&gt;S. 307&lt;/a&gt;: Prison Staff Safety Enhancement Act, which would &lt;a href="https://x.com/CraigCaplan/status/2094387093791199388?s=12&amp;amp;t=cV7b5R5rik4TqoNyck6DeQ"&gt;require the Department of Justice to establish national standards to prevent &amp;amp; reduce sexual harassment and assault against federal prison staff by inmates&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s858"&gt;S. 858&lt;/a&gt;: Hershel ‘Woody’ Williams National Medal of Honor Monument Location Act, which would authorize the National Medal of Honor Museum Foundation to establish a commemorative work on the National Mall to honor the extraordinary acts of valor, selfless service, and sacrifice displayed by Medal of Honor recipients&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr6500"&gt;H.R. 6500: Continuing Appropriations and Extensions Act, 2027&lt;/a&gt;, which would keep the government funded until December 11, 2026.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Yet Another Great Read from First Branch Forecast&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Our colleagues at the First Branch Forecast have a concise discussion this week of how the House’s power structures have evolved over its existence. It’s useful for both understanding what’s happening now and to envision different ways it can operate because there have been many ways it &lt;em&gt;has&lt;/em&gt; operated: &lt;a href="https://firstbranchforecast.substack.com/p/the-house-at-the-end-of-an-era"&gt;The House at the End of an Era&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;See everyone Friday to see what happened this week!&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>Legislative Preview</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Learn about government spending from this First Branch Forecast podcast interview</title>
      <author>Joshua Tauberer       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/620/2026-08-23_learn-about-government-spending-from-this-first-branch-forecast-podcast-interview</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/620/2026-08-23_learn-about-government-spending-from-this-first-branch-forecast-podcast-interview</guid>
      <pubDate>Sun, 23 Aug 2026 19:20:02 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;Our friend Chris Nehls at First Branch Forecast posted a podcast interview with two experts on how Congress directs the federal government to spend money.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Christie Wentworth of Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington, a government accountability watchdog organization, had this to say about it:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;[P]ower of the purse issues have historically, they have really great history of bipartisan support because it is an institutional concern. Members of Congress want their constituents to be supported and they want a federal government that that operates effectively and that does the things that Congress wants. And the money runs the world, right? And so this is the most powerful tool that that the legislative branch has to give money to the executive branch to make it do things, and to keep money from the executive branch to prevent it from doing things. There has been a lot of really positive engagement in this area in the past. I hope it continues into the future.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;She and Cerin Lindgrensavage of Protect Democracy discuss a wide rage of processes and isssues, and you can learn far more than you ever knew you wanted to from their interview.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Here’s the link to listen or read:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://firstbranchforecast.substack.com/p/interview-cerin-lindgrensavage-and"&gt;https://firstbranchforecast.substack.com/p/interview-cerin-lindgrensavage-and&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>Analysis and Commentary</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>House passes congressional stock buying ban</title>
      <author>Brandi M. Vail       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/619/2026-08-12_house-passes-congressional-stock-buying-ban</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/619/2026-08-12_house-passes-congressional-stock-buying-ban</guid>
      <pubDate>Wed, 12 Aug 2026 19:17:38 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;On July 22 the House passed the &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr7008"&gt;Stop Insider Trading Act&lt;/a&gt; (SITA), with a poison pill for the Senate. The main part of the legislation would &lt;strong&gt;increase penalties for insider trading in Congress&lt;/strong&gt; from $200 to $2000 or 10% of the value of the transaction, whichever is greater, plus any profits. But the current penalties which are imposed by the &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/112/s2038"&gt;STOCK Act&lt;/a&gt;, passed in 2012, have &lt;a href="https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4705069"&gt;never been successfully enforced&lt;/a&gt;, despite violations. SITA would also ban legislators, their spouses, and dependents &lt;strong&gt;from purchasing individual stocks&lt;/strong&gt;, but it would not require them to divest any stocks they currently own. It also would &lt;strong&gt;not ban them from selling stocks they already own&lt;/strong&gt;, as long as they issue a public notice at least seven days in advance.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Critics claim it &lt;a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/22/house-congressional-stock-trading.html"&gt;doesn’t go far enough&lt;/a&gt; to stop insider trading, as its name implies.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;But the bill is probably dead in the Senate because of a poison pill: SITA also includes &lt;a href="https://rules.house.gov/sites/evo-subsites/republicans-rules.house.gov/files/documents/bill-to-bill_bills-119hr7008rh-hr9368rh_to_rcp2-119-38-stopinsidertrading.pdf"&gt;most of&lt;/a&gt;  &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr9368"&gt;H.R. 9368: Voter ID Act&lt;/a&gt;, which would require photo ID to cast a vote in federal elections.&lt;/strong&gt; The addition comes as &lt;a href="https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/trump-save-america-act-congress-voting-restriction-bill-what-know-rcna587813"&gt;President Trump continues to pressure the Senate&lt;/a&gt; to pass the SAVE America Act, which would require photo ID and proof of citizenship to vote in federal elections and require states to submit voter rolls to a federal database for review. Rep.Thomas Massie, a Republican who voted for SITA, posted on &lt;a href="https://x.com/RepThomasMassie?lang=en"&gt;social media&lt;/a&gt;, complaining that Republicans inserted this provision to SITA with the intention of using it to dissuade Democrats from voting for the bill so it can be used against them in the upcoming November midterms. Still, 13 Democrats voted with Republicans to advance the bill to the Senate. Just as the SAVE America Act does not yet have the votes to pass the Senate, with Democrats opposing it, SITA probably won’t either.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Insider trading in Congress and the White House has been a public concern for a long time, and there is bipartisan support to address it. &lt;strong&gt;Other bills have been proposed by both parties this Congress, but haven’t gained as much ground.&lt;/strong&gt; The &lt;a href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/5106/text"&gt;Restore Trust in Congress Act&lt;/a&gt; sponsored by Rep. Chip Roy (R), the &lt;a href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/6731/text?s=1&amp;amp;r=14"&gt;Restore Trust in Government Act&lt;/a&gt; sponsored by Rep. Seth Magaziner (D), and the &lt;a href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/1498/text"&gt;Halting Ownership and Non-Ethical Stock Trading (HONEST) Act&lt;/a&gt; sponsored by Rep. Josh Hawley (R) each would have provided stronger enforcement against government insider trading than SITA. None of those bills, however, have made it to either chamber floor for a vote.&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>Bill Summary</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Preserve the records of the Surveys &amp; Investigations of the House Appropriations committee</title>
      <author>Joshua Tauberer       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/618/2026-08-11_preserve-the-records-of-the-surveys-investigations-of-the-house-appropriations-committee</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/618/2026-08-11_preserve-the-records-of-the-surveys-investigations-of-the-house-appropriations-committee</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 11 Aug 2026 15:16:23 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;Last week we signed onto a &lt;a href="https://americalabs.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/2026-08-07-Coalition-Letter-on-S-I.pdf"&gt;letter to the chair and ranking member of the House Committee on Appropriations&lt;/a&gt; regarding the records of the committee’s Surveys &amp;amp; Investigations from 1943 to 2024:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“The Surveys &amp;amp; Investigations … was designed to provide the Appropriations Committee with a dedicated, nonpartisan investigative capacity to supplement agency testimony and inform the committee’s appropriations and oversight work. Over the ensuing decades, S&amp;amp;I investigators—detailed from the Federal Bureau of Investigation and the Government Accountability Office, as well as other federal entities—wrote thousands of reports at the direction of appropriations subcommittees. These investigations constituted a significant portion of the committee’s work and were generated as a result of written tasking letters signed by the chair and ranking members of the full committee and appropriate appropriations subcommittee.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“Preserving these records is not merely a question of compliance, but of stewardship. They document a rare and long-standing oversight function of the House Appropriations Committee and will remain an important resource for future Congresses and for the House as an institution. They provide important insight into how congressional oversight has functioned over time and how specific investigations have shaped national policy and governance.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;We asked the committee to ensure the S&amp;amp;I records are preserved and eventually made available to the public.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;For more information, see &lt;a href="https://matthewdull.substack.com/p/oversight-out-of-sight-document-set"&gt;Oversight Out of Sight&lt;/a&gt;, an explanation from colleague Matthew Dull.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;We routinely sign onto letters to legislators in Congress advocating for more transparency and accountability in government. We don’t take sides on issues on before Congress &lt;em&gt;except&lt;/em&gt; issues like this that relate to our mission..&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>News About GovTrack</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>It's Different in the Senate Part Two</title>
      <author>Amy West       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/617/2026-08-08_its-different-in-the-senate-part-two</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/617/2026-08-08_its-different-in-the-senate-part-two</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 08 Aug 2026 18:35:52 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;Last week, while noting that it’s often as important to look at bills that fail on cloture (aka filibuster) or passage in the Senate, we also noted that two items did actually make it through the Senate for presumed final votes this week. And that’s sort of what happened. Nothing the Senate passed this week is close to becoming law; the House will still have to agree to the passed legislation in its current form.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Group Confirmation of Nominees&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;The big group of nominees whose confirmation was set up last week were successfully confirmed this week &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s225"&gt;51-47&lt;/a&gt; with a party line vote.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;H.R. 5334 Passes, but SEED Act does not&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;What does this mean? It means that the Senate struck the existing legislative language and replaced it with new language about sanctions against Russia. Now the bill is &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr5334/text"&gt;H.R. 5334: Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026&lt;/a&gt;. This new version passed the Senate &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s224"&gt;86-11&lt;/a&gt;. Now it goes back to the House for them to vote again.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Why would the Senate do it this way? If the bill has already gone through the House already, then substituting new text is less procedurally complicated than starting from scratch.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;There’s a New Attorney General and Director of the CDC&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;On the Nomination PN1078: Todd Blanche, of Florida, to be Attorney General, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s230"&gt;50-49&lt;/a&gt; at 4:17 am August 8.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;On the Nomination PN932: Erica Schwartz, of Florida, to be Director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s220"&gt;51-44&lt;/a&gt; Wednesday, August 5th in the afternoon.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Nationwide Voter ID fails in the Senate&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;While the Senate still hasn’t held any more votes on the SAVE America Act because it still hasn’t got enough votes to get past the filibuster or even pass by the lower simple majority required for passage, the Senate did have a filibuster/cloture vote on a brand new bill introduced Wednesday, August 5th: &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s5271"&gt;S. 5271: A bill to amend the Help America Vote Act of 2002 to require voters to provide photo identification&lt;/a&gt;. That vote, which needed 60 yes votes, failed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s231"&gt;52-46&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;This bill, had it passed both the Senate and the House, would become effective the day after passage and signing by the President. Since the House is out until September, &lt;em&gt;that&lt;/em&gt; means that Congress would have made a major change in voting practices nationwide just weeks before an election. It would have been extremely difficult and likely impossible for all 50 states to change their existing laws, train all of their poll workers, notify all voters and change all of their signage for all polling locations in time.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Senate’s Continuing Resolution (for government funding) Passes&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.congress.gov/crs-appropriations-status-table"&gt;No regular appropriations bills have passed both chambers of Congress yet&lt;/a&gt; and the fiscal year ends on September 30th.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The House left for its August recess two weeks ago and returns August 31st. The Senate is, as of today, August 8th, out until September 14th.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr9770"&gt;House passed its version of a continuing resolution&lt;/a&gt; to keep the government funded and avoid a shut down on July 21st.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The Senate is skipping that version entirely and passed its own &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s228"&gt;90-6&lt;/a&gt; at 3:37 am on Saturday, August 8th.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Once again the Senate used an existing bill that was part of the way through the legislative process to speed things up. Unlike with the SEED Act though, they kept at least some of &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr6500/text/eas#link=B_2008&amp;amp;nearest=id0cc3b91ef138417b9ecabcdd18bed626"&gt;the old text&lt;/a&gt; while adding the continuing resolution provisions.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Thus, what is still called &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr6500"&gt;H.R. 6500: AGOA Extension Act&lt;/a&gt; is actually now a continuing resolution primarily about keeping the government funded.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;According to the &lt;a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/08/us/politics/senate-passes-stopgap-bill-shutdown.html?unlocked_article_code=1.31A.qPT7.3f9LDGGzvUOS&amp;amp;smid=url-share"&gt;New York Times&lt;/a&gt;, the continuing resolution prohibits any transfers of funds to the Border Patrol and pushes back by one month the start of a ban on intoxicating hemp products.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Obviously, there’s going to be more fighting between the two chambers over what finally gets passed, if anything, to prevent another government shut down. While &lt;a href="https://pressgallery.house.gov/schedules/2026-house-calendar"&gt;the House returns on August 31st&lt;/a&gt;, the Senate won’t be back until &lt;a href="https://www.senate.gov/legislative/resources/pdf/2026_calendar.pdf"&gt;September 14&lt;/a&gt;. That leaves two weeks to find a compromise that can pass both chambers. You can reasonably expect that if such a compromise exists, it won’t pass both chambers until the very end of September.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;We’ll be using the next few weeks to write about other Congress-related topics. See you all next time.&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>Legislative Recap</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>It's Different in the Senate</title>
      <author>Amy West       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/616/2026-07-31_its-different-in-the-senate</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/616/2026-07-31_its-different-in-the-senate</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 31 Jul 2026 15:46:55 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;We regularly note that the Senate moves more slowly than the House. This week, with the House out of session, we have a nice short set of votes to demonstrate why this is the case.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The Senate held eight votes this week. Of those eight, one was a final vote that was not procedural in nature. At least, officially it was one of eight.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;But that’s the thing: in the Senate the vast majority of items require that at some point a piece of legislation garner at least 60 votes. Usually the 60 vote threshold is for some type of procedural vote. But, because a failure to clear that threshold brings work on the legislation to a halt, at least for a while, that 60 vote threshold can serve as a functional final vote. The main source of exceptions to the 60 vote threshold are nominations and certain privileged resolutions that only require a simple majority.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Director of National Intelligence&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;The one thing that affirmatively happened this week was that an administration nominee was confirmed:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s210"&gt;Motion to Invoke Cloture:&lt;/a&gt; Walter Clayton to be Director of National Intelligence: Walter Clayton, of New York, to be Director of National Intelligence passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s210"&gt;51-43&lt;/a&gt;. “Cloture” (aka voting to end debate) is a type of procedural vote that usually requires 60 votes to pass. But this was cloture on a nomination, so it only needed 50 and just squeaked by with 51.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s211"&gt;On the Nomination PN1092: Walter Clayton, of New York, to be Director of National Intelligence&lt;/a&gt; passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s211"&gt;51-47&lt;/a&gt;. Like final votes on legislation, nominations only need a simple majority which, again, Republicans just managed for the new Director of National Intelligence.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;p&gt;If nominations still required 60 votes to end debate / achieve cloture, then Clayton would not have been confirmed.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In other words, the Senate’s structure gives the minority party considerable power to prevent action where the 60 vote threshold exists. Where it doesn’t, then sub-60-vote majorities can prevail.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;H.R. 5334: SEED Act&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr5334"&gt;H.R. 5334: SEED Act&lt;/a&gt;, which would amend the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 to allow early childhood educators to take the educator expense deduction and which passed the House in April had two procedural votes this week and both sailed over the 60 vote threshold. Next week, one would expect a vote on final passage which would mean the SEED Act would then go to the President for signing.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s212"&gt;Motion to Invoke Cloture on the Motion to Proceed to H.R. 5334&lt;/a&gt; passed 86-12 on Tuesday.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s213"&gt;Motion to Proceed on H.R. 5334: SEED Act&lt;/a&gt; passed 84-12 on Wednesday.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;h2&gt;En Bloc Nominations&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;This year the Senate approved a new and slightly clunky method of approving nominations in a group (“en bloc”) without having to reach a 60 vote threshold. Once again, there were multiple procedural votes to set up for a final vote on the nominations themselves next week:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s214"&gt;Motion to Invoke Cloture: S. Res. 817&lt;/a&gt; passed 50-45 on Wednesday. This vote ended debate on a resolution that authorizes voting on nominees en bloc. It only needed a simple majority and just barely got it.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s217"&gt;S.Res. 817: An executive resolution authorizing the en bloc consideration in Executive Session of certain nominations on the Executive Calendar&lt;/a&gt; passed 50-47 on Thursday.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Needed a Simple Majority and Didn’t Get It&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Finally, the last two votes of the week were still procedural and they only needed a simple majority, but they did not get it in either case.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The first was a vote on a motion to proceed to a Congressional disapproval of a Labor Department regulation. It failed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s215"&gt;47-52&lt;/a&gt;. All Democrats voted for it; all Republicans against and the Republicans have the majority, so that’s that.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The second was a motion to discharge a resolution against the Iran War. Motions to discharge only need a simple majority in the Senate. However, while three Republicans voted to discharge the resolution, one Democrat voted against and it failed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s216"&gt;49-50&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;So, that’s all eight votes in the Senate this week. It might not seem like much happened, but it did because in the Senate, it’s at least as much about stopping legislation / nominations as it is passing any of them.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;There’s a new DNI, next week the Senate will confirm more nominees and likely provide the final vote to make the SEED Act law.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;See everyone next Friday when hopefully we have more action from the Senate to report.&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>Legislative Recap</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>House Votes Galore</title>
      <author>Amy West       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/615/2026-07-24_house-votes-galore</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/615/2026-07-24_house-votes-galore</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 24 Jul 2026 14:33:27 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;The House is now &lt;a href="https://pressgallery.house.gov/schedules/2026-house-calendar"&gt;out until August 31st&lt;/a&gt;, ostensibly for a “district work period” and campaigning.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;(The Senate will remain in town for &lt;a href="https://www.senate.gov/legislative/resources/pdf/2026_calendar.pdf"&gt;the next two weeks&lt;/a&gt;.)&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Before leaving Washington, the House held quite a few votes, including a few that set up funding vehicles for later. No votes resulted in new laws this week. Everything below will have to go through the Senate.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Future Funding&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Because of various additions to each of these bills for &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s1383"&gt;SAVE America Act provisions&lt;/a&gt; and other Republican priorities, these bills will, once the Senate considers them, almost certainly be amended and have to come back to the House for another vote. They are not anywhere near becoming law.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr9770"&gt;H.R. 9770: Making continuing appropriations for fiscal year 2027&lt;/a&gt;, which will provide federal funding for all agencies that need it through December 4, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h272"&gt;220-205&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hconres113"&gt;H.Con.Res. 113&lt;/a&gt;: Establishing the congressional budget for the United States Government for fiscal year 2027 and setting forth the appropriate budgetary levels for fiscal years 2028 through 2036, which is step one in yet another reconciliation bill process, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h281"&gt;216-214&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr8800"&gt;H.R. 8800: National Defense Authorization Act for Fiscal Year 2027&lt;/a&gt;, which provides the framework for what Congress would like to see done with national defense appropriations, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h278"&gt;216-212&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Other Votes&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr1118"&gt;H.R. 1118: Value Over Cost Act of 2026&lt;/a&gt;, which would change the government’s purchasing approach so that lowest cost bids would not always be the deciding factor in competitive bidding (per &lt;a href="https://www.cbo.gov/publication/62247"&gt;CBO&lt;/a&gt;), passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h252"&gt;421-1&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr8823"&gt;H.R. 8823: Putting Patients First by Strengthening Provider Accountability in FECA Act&lt;/a&gt;, which would amend the Federal Employees’ Compensation Act to allow the Secretary of Labor to suspend payments to medical providers who have been convicted of fraud, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h251"&gt;394-0&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr4541"&gt;H.R. 4541: To reauthorize the Young Women’s Breast Health Education and Awareness Requires Learning Young Act of 2009&lt;/a&gt; passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h250"&gt;394-6&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr6955"&gt;H.R. 6955: Main Street Capital Access Act&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IN12671.html"&gt;a bank deregulation bill&lt;/a&gt;, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h271"&gt;270-155&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr7008"&gt;H.R. 7008: Stop Insider Trading Act&lt;/a&gt; passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h280"&gt;232-198&lt;/a&gt;. Per &lt;a href="https://rollcall.com/2026/07/16/curbing-congress-stock-trading-partisan-turn/"&gt;Roll Call&lt;/a&gt;, it would “prohibit members of Congress, their spouses and dependent children from buying new stocks. It would allow them to keep the ones they already own, as well as buy and sell diversified investment funds.” Tacked onto the end of the bill is &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr7008"&gt;a provision to require photo ID to vote nationwide&lt;/a&gt;. This bill is unlikely to pass the Senate, especially with the voter ID provision added to it.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr8884"&gt;H.R. 8884: Removing Barriers to Work for Disabled Americans Act&lt;/a&gt;, which would (per &lt;a href="https://www.cbo.gov/publication/62545"&gt;CBO&lt;/a&gt;) reauthorize the Social Security Administration (SSA) to conduct Disability Insurance (DI) demonstration projects through calendar year 2031 and allow SSA to temporarily change how benefit amounts are calculated for participants in demonstration projects, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h283"&gt;232-188&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hconres89"&gt;H.Con.Res. 89&lt;/a&gt;: Directing the President, pursuant to section 5(c) of the War Powers Resolution, to remove United States Armed Forces from hostilities with Iran, which does not force any action from either the Senate or the President, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h282"&gt;214-208&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Senate&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/sjres180"&gt;S.J.Res. 180&lt;/a&gt;: A joint resolution to direct the removal of United States Armed Forces from hostilities within or against the Islamic Republic of Iran that have not been authorized by Congress, which would (if it passed the Senate) have potentially forced the President to act, did not make it through a procedural vote, failing &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s207"&gt;47-49&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;p&gt;On Bluesky, &lt;a href="https://bsky.app/profile/jbendery.bsky.social/post/3mrcyhgfmj22e"&gt;Jen Bendery, senior politics reporter at Huffpost&lt;/a&gt;, provided a nice explanation of the difference between a concurrent resolution (H.Con.Res. 89 above) and a joint resolution (S.J.Res. 180 above) which would go to the House like any other bill.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;See you all next week assuming there’s any votes to report from the Senate.&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>Legislative Recap</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>$35 Insulin For (Mostly) All?</title>
      <author>Amy West       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/614/2026-07-20_35-insulin-for-mostly-all</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/614/2026-07-20_35-insulin-for-mostly-all</guid>
      <pubDate>Mon, 20 Jul 2026 14:46:02 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/117/hr5376"&gt;Inflation Reduction Act of 2022&lt;/a&gt; capped the cost of insulin for Medicare beneficiaries at $35. It went into effect in 2023. Now &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s4189"&gt;S. 4189: INSULIN Act of 2026&lt;/a&gt;, a bipartisan bill with a &lt;a href="http://v"&gt;hearing scheduled for Wednesday&lt;/a&gt;, would expand the $35 cap to everyone with private insurance as well as (&lt;a href="https://www.politico.com/news/2026/06/24/republicans-debate-insulin-price-cap-00972910?utm_source=dlvr.it&amp;amp;utm_medium=bluesky"&gt;per Politico&lt;/a&gt;) create a $100 million dollar grant program to guarantee $35 insulin to uninsured people. If enacted, it would expand significantly the pool of people who pay no more than $35 a month for insulin, but there would likely still be gaps.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Who Needs Insulin?&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://usdss.cdc.gov/diabetes/report.html"&gt;12% of the U.S. population&lt;/a&gt; may have &lt;a href="https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/diabetes"&gt;diabetes&lt;/a&gt;. In Type 1 diabetes, the pancreas simply doesn’t make insulin and daily insulin injections are needed to survive. In Type 2 diabetes, which is far more common,, the body produces insulin but can’t make good use of what’s there, and insulin injections may be a part of treatment.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Didn’t They Do This Already?&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Despite being cheap to produce, &lt;a href="https://healthstreet.program.ufl.edu/wordpress/files/2018/12/The-cost-of-Insulin.pdf"&gt;costs for insulin rose dramatically in the early to mid 2000s&lt;/a&gt;. While the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022 does appear to have led to &lt;a href="https://www.goodrx.com/healthcare-access/research/how-much-does-insulin-cost-compare-brands?srsltid=AfmBOop0ZGPQE_C0u968U9tZ51bXOdR5OvVxMkvA7qyRyNtBEG1NTW64"&gt;further voluntary price reductions&lt;/a&gt; from manufacturers, for everyone not on Medicare, it can still be quite expensive. People who have private commercial insurance (either from their employers or on their own) also face &lt;a href="https://diabetes.org/tools-resources/affordable-insulin/state-insulin-copay-caps"&gt;considerable variation in co-pay costs by state&lt;/a&gt; depending on what kind of plan their employer has selected. Medicaid patients generally pay no more than $4/month for insulin, but the only recourse for fully uninsured people are drug company patient assistance programs, &lt;a href="https://diabetes.org/tools-resources/affordable-insulin"&gt;for which not everyone may qualify&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Advocacy&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Is this an issue you feel strongly about? There’s plenty of time to get involved, both to see the bill passed as is or to offer constructive changes. You can see how the Senate Health, Education, Labor and Pensions Committee is feeling about it when it has &lt;a href="https://www.congress.gov/event/119th-congress/senate-event/338667?s=3&amp;amp;r=11"&gt;a hearing on this bill and host of others this coming Wednesday&lt;/a&gt;. The bill is likely to make it out of committee, but it would still have to pass the Senate and then the House, which are far from guaranteed.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;If your Senators are not on the relevant committees or already cosponsors, you can ask them to consider co-sponsoring it to help build support, as well asking them to vote yes if it comes to the Senate Floor. &lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;With respect to your representatives in the House, you can contact them/their staff in writing or in person to say you’d like to see a House version of this bill or to ask them to support this one when it comes to the House.&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>Bill Summary</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>No voting machine was hacked: What we know from what President Trump didn't say in his address</title>
      <author>Joshua Tauberer       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/613/2026-07-17_no-voting-machine-was-hacked-what-we-know-from-what-president-trump-didnt-say-in-his-address</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/613/2026-07-17_no-voting-machine-was-hacked-what-we-know-from-what-president-trump-didnt-say-in-his-address</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 17 Jul 2026 15:55:16 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I was expecting to hear that the outcome of a specific election was in question. Instead, the President listed failed and hypothetical threats and offered no new solutions.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;No votes were altered&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Here’s the bottom line of Trump’s speech &lt;a href="https://www.foxnews.com/politics/trump-releases-declassified-election-intelligence-says-reveals-shocking-vulnerabilities"&gt;according to Fox News&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Trump did not claim China changed votes or altered election results.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;No votes were altered in recent elections, according to what the President &lt;em&gt;didn’t&lt;/em&gt; say.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“We know that they can change voter registration and your vote. We know it’s possible,” Department of Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin said this morning. He offered no details, and did not say that any voter registrations or votes &lt;em&gt;were&lt;/em&gt; changed.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The President also mentioned China’s “attempt to manufacture illegal ballots for Joe Biden.” Based on what he didn’t say, the attempt presumably failed.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The President described a world in which foreign powers are trying to hack election systems. That’s true and concerning. But at least when it came to altering votes in past elections, any attempts failed. (He did mention votes being changed — only a thousand miles away in Venezuela.)&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;No mass non-citizen voting&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;He also discussed finding “278,000 non-citizens who are registered to vote” in California and three other Democratic states.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;But the President did not say that they voted, and although fraudulently registering to vote is illegal and one of his top priorities, he still did not announce any prosecutions&lt;/strong&gt;. DHS Secretary Mullin promised this morning that they would look at who votes in the 2026 elections later this year and pursue “prison time” for state officials who do not &lt;a href="https://www.brennancenter.org/topics/voting-elections/vote-suppression/voter-purges"&gt;purge&lt;/a&gt; voter rolls.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.foxnews.com/politics/quarter-million-noncitizens-registered-vote-4-key-states-dhs-alleges"&gt;Fox News described the “278,000” finding as a “preliminary review.”&lt;/a&gt; That’s probably because there is no direct correlation between voter registration records and immigration records. &lt;a href="https://electioninnovation.org/research/claims-noncitizen-registration-voting-analysis-tracker/"&gt;Name mixups, naturalization, and states registering people who didn’t ask to be registered&lt;/a&gt; are common.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Expect only a small fraction of that count to turn out to be voter registration fraud, and an even smaller fraction of that might have improperly voted — far, far fewer than could alter the outcome of an election.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The SAVE America Act might prevent these improper non-citizen voter registrations in the future, but until Trump finds mass non-citizen &lt;em&gt;voting,&lt;/em&gt; the bill’s most significant impact would be to &lt;a href="https://www.ncsl.org/state-legislatures-news/details/9-things-to-know-about-the-proposed-save-america-act"&gt;make voter registration harder for married women&lt;/a&gt; (or anyone else) who changed their name.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Breach of voter registration information&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;The President also disclosed intelligence that China had obtained 220 million voter files and that election systems are “vulnerable and they’re easily compromised.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;As one of our readers wrote to us this morning, every computer is hackable. That’s not being flippant. It’s a deeply serious concern, and it’s unfortunately not a shocker anymore when a major data breach like this is announced.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;And yet:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;The President did not announce restoring &lt;a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2025/04/05/us/politics/trump-loomer-haugh-cyberattacks-elections.html"&gt;funding he cut for cybersecurity assistance&lt;/a&gt; for state and local elections officials.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;The President did not announce appointing &lt;a href="https://www.votebeat.org/national/2026/07/09/trump-fires-election-assistance-commission-members-hicks-hovland-mccormick/"&gt;bipartisan officials to the Election Assistance Commission&lt;/a&gt;, restarting the agency, to push higher standards for election systems.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The President offered no policy proposal to prevent a future data breach or hack.&lt;/strong&gt; In his speech this morning, DHS Secretary Mullin referred to a policy announced last week that &lt;a href="https://www.dhs.gov/news/2026/07/10/dhs-requires-states-adopt-common-sense-election-security-measures-receiving-federal"&gt;grants to prevent or respond to terrorism would be withheld from states&lt;/a&gt; that don’t comply with new election security demands. Mullin also promised a new election infrastructure security plan in 30 days.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Fortunately, the President &lt;em&gt;didn’t say&lt;/em&gt; that any voter registrations had been altered. And according to an analysis of the newly declassified reports by &lt;a href="https://www.justsecurity.org/147633/trump-election-interference-speech/"&gt;Just Security&lt;/a&gt;, the President’s claims about hacked voter files may be overblown:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;the documents reviewed thus far are too heavily redacted to show how the claim involving 220 million voter files was derived, how the data was obtained, &lt;strong&gt;how much came from protected election systems rather than public or commercial sources&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;Voter registration information is widely circulated already. Every candidate and party already gets access to voter information. That’s how they know whose doors to knock on and who to target with mailings.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In fact, DHS Secretary Mullin said they would enforce voter fraud laws using “public records” of who voted, in his speech this morning. Voter registration information is, unfortunately, not much of a secret.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;And much of the same information has already been stolen from commercial sources, &lt;a href="https://www.ftc.gov/enforcement/refunds/equifax-data-breach-settlement"&gt;like the 2017 Equifax breach of credit information of 147 million people&lt;/a&gt;, or can be purchased from data brokers. China could have gotten voters’ information any number of ways, unfortunately.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;China worked to prevent Trump’s election&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://substack.govtrack.us/p/foreign-powers-are-trying-to-interfere"&gt;We said that Wednesday&lt;/a&gt;. It was known since the first Trump Administration that China was attempting to influence U.S. elections against Trump.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The President conveniently didn’t mention the efforts by Russia or Iran, much of which was in support of Trump’s reelection. Foreign influence is a national problem, not a partisan one.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Yet the President did not address why he &lt;a href="https://apnews.com/article/trump-election-security-fbi-cisa-foreign-interference-98f1e17c8a6d5923db945a27f06458e7"&gt;closed the Foreign Influence Task Force&lt;/a&gt; at the Department of Justice early last year, and he did not offer any new solutions to combat China’s foreign influence.&lt;/strong&gt; To the contrary, he downplays foreign influence when it helps him, as in last night’s address.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;A coverup and Michigan voter registration fraud&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;The President also discussed government employees burying some of this information from his briefings and “fraud by a large scale voter registration operation in Michigan”.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;If these were true and as serious as the President implied, one would expect that the President would have announced that individuals were already fired or announced unsealed criminal indictments. He did neither, promising those actions in the uncertain future.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;hr&gt; &lt;p&gt;The threat of foreign influence and interference &lt;a href="https://substack.govtrack.us/p/foreign-powers-are-trying-to-interfere"&gt;is real and has been happening for years&lt;/a&gt;, as we wrote Wednesday. Both federal and state officials work to keep elections secure, and that must continue. But President Trump’s speech last night shows that our elections have been successful &lt;em&gt;despite&lt;/em&gt; those threats — at least so far — and offered no new solutions to the very real cybersecurity threats we face.&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>The White House</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>The Republican In-Fighting Continues Except for When it Doesn't</title>
      <author>Amy West       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/612/2026-07-17_the-republican-in-fighting-continues-except-for-when-it-doesnt</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/612/2026-07-17_the-republican-in-fighting-continues-except-for-when-it-doesnt</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 17 Jul 2026 14:04:25 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;Once again, in-fighting in the House GOP resulted in them leaving town early this week. But there were votes! Some with big passing margins. Also, some related old business and a tiny preview of one bill for next week.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;House Votes&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;None of these bills are near to becoming law. They would all have to be passed in the Senate first.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr3106"&gt;H.R. 3106: Weatherizing Infrastructure in the North and Terrorism Emergency Readiness Act of 2025&lt;/a&gt;, which would require the Secretary of Homeland Security to conduct a collective response to a terrorism exercise that includes the management of cascading effects on critical infrastructure during times of extreme cold weather, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h234"&gt;400-7&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr8897"&gt;H.R. 8897: Improving Travel for American Families Act&lt;/a&gt;, which would allow TSA to design and implement a pilot program to speed up security screening for adults traveling with children, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h235"&gt;398-12&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr139"&gt;H.R. 139: Sunshine Protection Act of 2025&lt;/a&gt;, which would end the twice yearly clock changes, make Daylight Saving Time (DST) the default and allow states to choose permanent Standard Time instead, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h238"&gt;308-117&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2022/03/15/us/politics/daylight-saving-time-senate.html?eafs_enabled=false"&gt;A similar bill passed by unanimous consent in the Senate in 2022&lt;/a&gt;. In 2022, the House declined to take up the bill. Now, &lt;a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/14/us/politics/house-daylight-savings-time-sunshine-protection-act.html"&gt;the Senate is indicating it doesn’t have much interest&lt;/a&gt;. Because permanent DST would move sunrises later by an hour in the winter, it would &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/07/16/g-s1-133961/why-orthodox-jews-are-opposing-the-new-daylight-saving-bill-in-congress"&gt;create difficulties for anyone whose religious practices require morning daylight&lt;/a&gt;, like Orthodox Jews.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr1181"&gt;H.R. 1181: Protecting Privacy in Purchases Act&lt;/a&gt;, which would prohibit payment card networks and covered entities from requiring the use of or assigning merchant category codes that distinguish a firearms retailer from general-merchandise retailer or sporting-goods retailer, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h240"&gt;221-201&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr8595"&gt;H.R. 8595: National Security, Department of State, and Related Programs Appropriations Act, 2027&lt;/a&gt; passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h247"&gt;217-209&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;h2&gt;The Cancelled Vote&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Normally we wouldn’t mention a failed procedural vote, but this time it’s on a bill that’s worth knowing about and illustrative of the ongoing challenges to passing ostensibly Republican priorities. The bill is the &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr9237"&gt;H.R. 9237: Take Care of America’s Veterans Act&lt;/a&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2026/07/16/congress/house-veterans-benefits-bill-01000747?utm_source=dlvr.it&amp;amp;utm_medium=bluesky"&gt;which would apparently increase future retirement benefits by assigning the savings from decreased future disability benefits to retirement&lt;/a&gt;. This was problematic enough to keep a key number of Republicans from supporting the bill and the “motion to recommit” (the procedural vote) failed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h249"&gt;210-211&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;It’s possible that the same veteran could see reduced disability benefits for tinnitus (one of the conditions to be affected) but increased retirement benefits because they were in combat and come out even. But, obviously, there would be many instances in which the veteran needing disability support hadn’t been in combat and vice versa. This would lead to some veterans losing benefits while others gained. You can see why this would be a politically tricky vote. The Politico article linked above noted that the changes to disability coverage were first broached in the Biden Administration and may go ahead regardless. So, while the vote patterns here look highly partisan, the reality of managing benefits for veterans is probably a bit more complicated.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;A Second Bill Becomes Law without Presidential Action&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Recently the President made a big production of refusing to sign &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr6644"&gt;the housing bill&lt;/a&gt; passed in June. The President also refused to take any action on &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s629"&gt;S. 629: Emergency Conservation Program Improvement Act of 2025&lt;/a&gt;, which amends the Agricultural Credit Act of 1978 to remove barriers to agricultural producers in accessing funds to carry out emergency measures under the emergency conservation program, thus delaying the date it became law until July 12. Since this is likely to happen increasingly often with this President, you can bookmark our &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/browse#current_status%5B%5D=32&amp;amp;sort=-current_status_date"&gt;Advanced Search for Legislation&lt;/a&gt; page with the “Enacted by 10 Day Rule” option selected to keep track.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;US Elections Are Fine Despite Best Efforts by Foreign Governments&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;We wrote in advance about what we expected the President to allude to, albeit misleadingly to support his lies about the 2020 election, in his speech on Thursday, July 16. His speech was the same old same old and we anticipated much of it. You’re much better off reading our post “&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/posts/611/2026-07-15_foreign-powers-are-trying-to-interfere-with-us-elections-but-voting-machines-havent-failed-us-yet"&gt;Foreign powers are trying to interfere with U.S. elections, but voting machines haven’t failed us yet&lt;/a&gt;”. Also, &lt;a href="https://www.eac.gov/help-america-vote"&gt;consider becoming a poll worker&lt;/a&gt;. When you see firsthand everything that goes into administering an election in just a single county, you see immediately how difficult (bordering on impossible) it would be to successfully alter the outcome of a presidential election across the necessary thousands of US counties.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;A Bill that Won’t Ban Stock Trading, but Does Include SAVE Act Elements Next Week&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;The last time the House was in session, some members, most vocally &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/members/anna_luna/456891"&gt;Rep. Anna Paulina Luna (R-FL13)&lt;/a&gt;, demanded that the &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s1383"&gt;SAVE America Act&lt;/a&gt; be attached to every bill that gets a vote in the House. That isn’t quite occurring, but most bills coming forward now have at least some of the SAVE America Act attached.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;This will true of the &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr7008"&gt;Stop Insider Trading Act&lt;/a&gt; when it comes up for a vote next week. Per &lt;a href="https://rollcall.com/2026/07/16/curbing-congress-stock-trading-partisan-turn/"&gt;Roll Call&lt;/a&gt;, it would “prohibit members of Congress, their spouses and dependent children from buying new stocks. It would allow them to keep the ones they already own, as well as buy and sell diversified investment funds.” So, it would not actually stop insider trading since anything legislators kept would still be a source of temptation. This bill already has significant opposition in the House and the Senate so adding elements of the SAVE America Act likely just dooms it in the Senate. Still, it’s supposed to come to the floor next week. We’ll see.&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>Legislative Recap</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Foreign powers are trying to interfere with U.S. elections, but voting machines haven't failed us yet</title>
      <author>Joshua Tauberer       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/611/2026-07-15_foreign-powers-are-trying-to-interfere-with-us-elections-but-voting-machines-havent-failed-us-yet</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/611/2026-07-15_foreign-powers-are-trying-to-interfere-with-us-elections-but-voting-machines-havent-failed-us-yet</guid>
      <pubDate>Wed, 15 Jul 2026 21:04:56 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;While we can’t know if the efforts by foreign powers have changed people’s minds, the evidence so far indicates they haven’t compromised the tools we use to count the votes.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;President Trump’s Thursday night address will reveal purportedly &lt;a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/us/trumps-give-speech-about-foreign-interference-2020-election-ms-now-reports-2026-07-13/"&gt;declassified intelligence about voting machine vulnerabilities&lt;/a&gt;, Reuters reported. That prompted us to take a look at what’s known about foreign interference in past U.S. elections and the safety of U.S. voting equipment, what President Trump is doing at two federal agencies that support elections, and what would happen if large scale foreign interference is discovered after the fact.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Foreign influence and interference&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;The threat of foreign interference is real, with cyberattacks, foreign propaganda, and straw donors all strategies being used by U.S. adversaries. This has been widely reported over recent years, so you’re probably familiar with it — in which case, jump to the voting machines section below. Otherwise, here’s a recap:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;During the 2016 presidential campaign between then-candidate Donald Trump and candidate Hilary Clinton, Russia &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_Democratic_National_Committee_email_leak"&gt;hacked and released damaging Democratic emails&lt;/a&gt; through WikiLeaks. That year &lt;a href="https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2017/07/donald-trumps-jrs-email-exchange/533244/"&gt;a “Russian government attorney”&lt;/a&gt; met with Donald Trump’s son Don Jr. to provide “information that would incriminate Hillary and her dealings with Russia,” according to emails sent before the meeting (which was the widely reported 2016 Trump Tower meeting). Later during the campaign, &lt;a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2019/01/25/us/politics/roger-stone-trump-mueller.html"&gt;WikiLeaks communicated&lt;/a&gt; with Trump advisor Roger Stone about the hacked emails before they were publicly leaked, and &lt;a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2017/11/13/us/politics/donald-trump-jr-wikileaks-emails-democrats.html"&gt;with Don Jr.&lt;/a&gt; about publicizing the hacked emails. &lt;a href="https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/grand-jury-indicts-12-russian-intelligence-officers-hacking-offenses-related-2016-election"&gt;12 Russian intelligence officials were indicted for the hack&lt;/a&gt; that exfiltrated the emails. Separately, an &lt;a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2019/01/08/us/politics/manafort-trump-campaign-data-kilimnik.html"&gt;individual connected to Russian intelligence&lt;/a&gt; messaged with Trump campaign chair Paul Manafort about polling data.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;(The Steele Dossier, opposition research commissioned by Clinton’s campaign to discredit Trump, especially around Trump’s relationship with Russia, and which was largely discredited, was leaked by U.S. journalists in 2017. But after unsubstantiated claims that it was a part of a Russian intelligence operation, the &lt;a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2022/10/18/us/politics/igor-danchenko-russia-acquittal-trump.html"&gt;Trump Administration’s prosecution of one of the sources failed&lt;/a&gt; when a jury acquitted the source on all charges. It wasn’t foreign interference.)&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In 2020, the Biden Administration concluded that&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.lawfaremedia.org/article/office-director-national-intelligence-issues-statement-foreign-interference-2020-election"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;foreign powers were seeking to influence the 2020 presidential election&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; &lt;strong&gt;on both sides&lt;/strong&gt;: “China prefers that President Trump … does not win reelection”, “Russia is using a range of measures to primarily denigrate former Vice President Biden,” and “Iran seeks to undermine U.S. democratic institutions.” &lt;a href="https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/two-iranian-nationals-charged-cyber-enabled-disinformation-and-threat-campaign-designed"&gt;Iranians were later charged&lt;/a&gt; in 2021 for threatening Democrats to vote for Trump, hacking a state voter registration website, and attempting to pin the hack on the Democratic Party. &lt;strong&gt;In 2024,&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_interference_in_the_2024_United_States_elections"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Iran attempted to discredit the Trump campaign&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; &lt;strong&gt;by stealing and leaking campaign documents.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In 2024, the Department of Justice indicted Russians for &lt;strong&gt;propping up an&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://apnews.com/article/russian-interference-presidential-election-influencers-trump-999435273dd39edf7468c6aa34fad5dd"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;American media company as a front for Russian propaganda&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;: It was a right-wing media company featuring well-known commentator Benny Johnson and others. &lt;a href="https://cdt.org/insights/countdown-to-the-midterms-reexamining-foreign-influence-in-2020-elections/"&gt;Russian agents were also sanctioned&lt;/a&gt; by the Treasury Department in 2020-2021 for influence operations.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;These operations mostly targeted public opinion. Media manipulation, including through social media and AI-generated answers, is likely to be a significant issue in the years ahead.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In 2019, individuals were also charged in a straw donor and laundering scheme to &lt;a href="https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/lev-parnas-and-igor-fruman-charged-conspiring-violate-straw-and-foreign-donor-bans"&gt;route money from a Russian national to U.S. state and federal political campaigns&lt;/a&gt;. (Lev Parnas, who separately worked with Trump to research allegations against President Joe Biden’s son Hunter, was one of the individuals &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2022/06/29/1108635408/lev-parnas-sentenced-20-months"&gt;convicted&lt;/a&gt; of the scheme.) In 2025, a Russian and an Uzbekistan man living in Florida &lt;a href="https://floridapolitics.com/archives/749746-uzbek-national-who-pleaded-guilty-to-voter-fraud-sentenced-to-time-served/"&gt;submitted 132 fraudulent voter registrations&lt;/a&gt; — they both pleaded guilty.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In a highly unusual move, former &lt;a href="https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/gabbard-says-trump-requested-her-presence-at-fbi-search-of-georgia-election-center"&gt;Director of National Intelligence Tulsi Gabbard attended an FBI raid&lt;/a&gt; of election ballots in Fulton County, Georgia early this year, suggesting a foreign connection to a crime. &lt;strong&gt;That might be the subject of Trump’s address.&lt;/strong&gt; Little more is known about the raid, and Gabbard resigned in June.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Voting machines have been secure, so far&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Hacking electronic voting machines became a national issue 20 years ago when a college classmate of mine demonstrated &lt;a href="https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2006/09/princeton-researchers-release-devastating-diebold-security-report"&gt;manipulating vote totals in voting machines made by Diebold in 2006&lt;/a&gt; (the machines were not part of an actual election).&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;But voting machines have come a long way since then, with paper records of votes and &lt;a href="https://www.ncsl.org/elections-and-campaigns/post-election-audits"&gt;audits&lt;/a&gt;, and hacking voting machines usually &lt;strong&gt;requires physical access to each machine&lt;/strong&gt;, precinct by precinct. It’s impractical to manipulate enough votes that way to change the outcome of an election. That’s the hope.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;And the evidence supports that, so far. A 2019-2020 &lt;a href="https://www.intelligence.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/sites-default-files-documents-report-volume1.pdf"&gt;report by Senate Republicans&lt;/a&gt; found that while the Russian government targeted U.S. elections infrastructure from 2014-2017, “The Committee has seen &lt;strong&gt;no evidence that any votes were changed&lt;/strong&gt; or that any voting machines were manipulated” by Russia.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Voting machine irregularity conspiracy theories have been thoroughly debunked:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://apnews.com/article/fox-news-dominion-lawsuit-trial-trump-2020-0ac71f75acfacc52ea80b3e747fb0afe"&gt;Fox News agreed to pay voting machine maker Dominion nearly $1 billion&lt;/a&gt; in 2023 to settle a defamation lawsuit over Fox personalities’ extensive fabrications that Dominion machines flipped votes from Trump to Biden in 2020. &lt;a href="https://www.smartmatic.com/us/lawsuit-updates/"&gt;Newsmax settled with Smartmatic&lt;/a&gt;, another voting machine maker, and admitted it lied about the company’s machines and ties to Venezuela. Smartmatic also &lt;a href="https://www.smartmatic.com/us/lawsuit-updates/"&gt;won a similar case against Mike Lindell and MyPillow for their lies&lt;/a&gt; and is in ongoing litigation with Fox.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Cases of tampering with voting equipment, at least the ones that are found out, are rare. In 2024, &lt;a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2024/08/12/us/politics/tina-peters-guilty-voting-machine-tampering.html"&gt;a Colorado county clerk was convicted of tampering with the county’s voting machines&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://apnews.com/article/sidney-powell-plea-deal-georgia-election-indictment-ec7dc601ad78d756643aa2544028e9f5"&gt;Trump advisor Sidney Powell pleaded guilty to a similar charge&lt;/a&gt; in Georgia. They were, after the election, fishing for evidence in support of Trump’s claim that the 2020 election was rigged.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In the two decades since the Diebold demonstration, most states have moved to paper-based systems with machinery used for tabulation and accessibility support, along with auditing election results.&lt;/strong&gt; As of 2025, only Louisiana uses direct recording electronic systems exclusively &lt;a href="https://ballotpedia.org/Voting_equipment_by_state"&gt;according to Ballotopedia&lt;/a&gt;. More on that below.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Although Trump has been &lt;a href="https://www.vice.com/en/article/donald-trump-rigged-election-timeline/"&gt;crying “rigged” since losing a state primary election in 2016&lt;/a&gt;, neither &lt;strong&gt;the Department of Justice nor any state has yet brought charges against anyone — foreign or domestic — for large scale election crimes of any sort&lt;/strong&gt;. That’s really all you need to know to see what’s real and what’s not. See if he announces an indictment Thursday night.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Trump hamstrings federal agencies that support elections&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;The threat on voting machines remains anyway. That’s why the U.S. Election Assistance Commission (EAC) in 2025 &lt;a href="https://www.eac.gov/news/2025/05/28/eac-commissioners-issue-policy-support-paper-based-and-auditable-voting-systems"&gt;recommended that states use “voting systems that are auditable&lt;/a&gt; and software-independent … that have a paper record of every vote, such as paper ballots. . . . &lt;strong&gt;While most jurisdictions already use voting systems that produce a paper record of every vote&lt;/strong&gt;, the EAC has adopted this policy to encourage the few remaining election offices to follow their lead.” The EAC is a legally-bipartisan federal agency that supports states in enhancing their voting systems through research and grants. In 2025, &lt;a href="https://www.congress.gov/crs_external_products/LSB/PDF/LSB11368/LSB11368.1.pdf"&gt;Trump issued an executive order directing the EAC&lt;/a&gt; to “protect the integrity” of elections (&lt;a href="https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/presidents-executive-order-elections-explained"&gt;ostensibly&lt;/a&gt;). Yet &lt;strong&gt;early this month Trump&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.votebeat.org/national/2026/07/09/trump-fires-election-assistance-commission-members-hicks-hovland-mccormick/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;fired all of the agency’s remaining commissioners&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;strong&gt;, leaving the agency unable to take any further action.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) within the Department of Homeland Security, which Trump created in 2017, &lt;a href="https://www.cisa.gov/topics/election-security"&gt;provides cybersecurity support to state and local elections officials&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;strong&gt;Last year, Trump&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2025/04/05/us/politics/trump-loomer-haugh-cyberattacks-elections.html"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;cut CISA’s funding for election security&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;strong&gt;, and&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://federalnewsnetwork.com/cybersecurity/2026/04/plankey-withdraws-as-cisa-nominee/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;the agency’s director position is vacant&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; &lt;strong&gt;with no one currently nominated for Senate confirmation.&lt;/strong&gt; (In a case brought by Republican states against CISA and other agencies for &lt;a href="https://www.justsecurity.org/97268/murthy-missouri-social-media-disinformation/"&gt;collaborating with social media companies to ban election-related (and other) misinformation&lt;/a&gt;, the Supreme Court ruled in 2024 against the Republican states deciding that CISA had not coerced the social media companies.)&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Early last year, the Trump Administration also &lt;a href="https://apnews.com/article/trump-election-security-fbi-cisa-foreign-interference-98f1e17c8a6d5923db945a27f06458e7"&gt;closed the Foreign Influence Task Force&lt;/a&gt; at the Department of Justice.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;What if something did happen&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;The best time to secure an election is before it starts. The next best time is to &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/posts/451/2024-10-31_what-to-expect-and-what-not-on-election-day-and-in-the-weeks-ahead"&gt;challenge a result in court before the counts are certified&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;When that’s not possible, individuals can be prosecuted for election-related crimes. Legislators in Congress can be expelled by a 2/3rds vote. The president can be impeached and removed by Congress. Or, more commonly, they can resign.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Back in 2012, former Rep. Michael Grimm was investigated for improperly seeking assistance from a foreign national in soliciting campaign contributions, among other crimes. Grimm resigned and served time in prison for unrelated tax fraud (for which Trump issued a pardon much later). In 2023 Rep. George Santos was expelled from Congress and in 2024 he pleaded guilty to wire fraud and identity theft after it was discovered that he had egregiously lied during his campaign and fraudulently solicited political contributions. (His federal sentence was later commuted by Trump.) In 2024, Sen. Bob Menendez was convicted of bribery and other charges, and then he resigned from the Senate. &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/misconduct"&gt;These recent cases are rare&lt;/a&gt;, however.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;There is no process of recall by voters for legislators in Congress or for the president.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;If foreign interference or improper vote tabulation is discovered, in which an elected legislator had no role, unprecedented questions would arise about the right response.&lt;/strong&gt; Did the interference definitively change the outcome of the election? How would we prove it? Are the legislator’s constituents best served by the legislator remaining in office or without any representation in Congress until the seat is next filled? Would vacating the seat be giving the foreign power the outcome they wanted? There won’t be any good answers. And the bottom line is that &lt;a href="https://substack.govtrack.us/p/how-republicans-might-disrupt-the"&gt;elections must be decided by the votes&lt;/a&gt;. Anything else wouldn’t be democracy.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;What you can do&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.eac.gov/help-america-vote"&gt;We recommend working in the next election&lt;/a&gt;. Poll workers are trained to administer elections. There’s nothing like seeing how the process really works to put any concerns you might have about voting integrity to rest. It’s certainly a better use of your time than listening to politicians and talking heads stoking fears.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Corrections: I originally wrote that “In 2020, the Biden Administration concluded that foreign powers were seeking to influence the 2020 presidential election on both sides.” But that report was at the end of the first Trump Administration. Apologies.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>The White House</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Strategery in the House</title>
      <author>Amy West       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/610/2026-07-02_strategery-in-the-house</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/610/2026-07-02_strategery-in-the-house</guid>
      <pubDate>Thu, 02 Jul 2026 16:55:46 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;The House continues its split personality. We said on Monday that there were &lt;a href="https://www.majorityleader.gov/schedule/weekly-schedule.htm"&gt;several bills with significant bipartisan support coming to the floor this week&lt;/a&gt; and that, if they weren’t derailed by internal House Republican conflicts over their own priorities, would likely pass. And they all did. Most by &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/browse#sort=-current_status_date&amp;amp;current_status%5B%5D=4,5,6,11,8,9,28,12,13,16,17,24"&gt;voice vote&lt;/a&gt;, but two by roll call:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr7128"&gt;H.R. 7128: TRIA Program Reauthorization Act of 2026&lt;/a&gt;, which would extend the Terrorism Risk Insurance program, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h229"&gt;373-15&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr7757"&gt;H.R. 7757: KIDS Act&lt;/a&gt; passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h228"&gt;267-117&lt;/a&gt;. If it passes the Senate, this bill will decrease all users’ privacy online and lead to online speech being curtailed in the name of protecting children. Per &lt;a href="https://rollcall.com/2026/06/29/kids-online-safety-push-clouded-by-house-senate-divide/"&gt;Roll Call&lt;/a&gt;, most of the Congressional conflict is about whether it does those things enough. From the perspective of privacy advocates, any version of this legislation does both too much. The &lt;a href="https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2026/06/kids-act-would-require-age-checks-get-online"&gt;Electronic Frontier Foundation&lt;/a&gt; provides an explanation of how the KIDS Act, even without the stronger Senate provisions, would do so.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;p&gt;There were also two votes on bills put forward via personal privilege, a mechanism that forces a bill to the floor regardless of whether House leadership wants it there or not. One passed; one failed:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hres1399"&gt;H.Res. 1399&lt;/a&gt;: Directing the Committee on Ethics to preserve and publicly release records relating to monetary settlements involving acts of sexual harassment passed unanimously &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h233"&gt;420-0&lt;/a&gt;. On July 2, &lt;a href="https://ethics.house.gov/press-releases/statement-of-the-committee-on-ethics-regarding-sexual-misconduct-settlements/"&gt;the Committee on Ethics published a statement saying they don’t have this information&lt;/a&gt; and therefore cannot release it.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hconres108"&gt;H.Con.Res. 108&lt;/a&gt;: Directing the President pursuant to section 5(c) of the War Powers Resolution to remove United States Armed Forces from hostilities in Lebanon failed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h232"&gt;189-235&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;p&gt;The four bills that were to go through the Rules Committee, a path reserved for legislation expected to get only Republican support (or maybe a very small number of Democratic votes; but not enough to pass via suspension), all are on hold. One is an &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr8595"&gt;appropriations bill for national security, the Department of State and related programs&lt;/a&gt;, one is about &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr8884"&gt;disabled workers&lt;/a&gt;, one is &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hres1383"&gt;a self-congratulatory resolution for last year’s reconciliation bill anniversary&lt;/a&gt; and the last is the &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/posts"&gt;National Defense Authorization Act (NDAA)&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;This is weird for several reasons: first, usually, the majority (regardless of party) &lt;em&gt;likes&lt;/em&gt; to get its own priorities passed. Even when there are factions within the majority who are unhappy, a path forward is found.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;And a path forward was found! The small group of Republican holdouts demanded that the &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s1383"&gt;SAVE America Act&lt;/a&gt; be added to the NDAA, and &lt;a href="https://x.com/JakeSherman/status/2072718086940037372"&gt;it was&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h231"&gt;Then they voted it against it anyway&lt;/a&gt;. Why?&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The stated reason is that SAVE could just be stripped out by the Senate. Which is true. Because, as far as your GovTracker is aware, there is no way for the House to structure a bill such that the Senate cannot amend it. But at least one person in this small group seems to think there’s One Weird Trick that House leadership refuses to try and so, when the rule to govern debate on the Republican priority bills came to the floor, it was voted down, taking the other bills with it.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;As of Thursday, July 2, &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/members/anna_luna/456891"&gt;Rep. Luna (R-FL13)&lt;/a&gt;, who has been the main House spokesperson for the folks trying to force Senate passage of SAVE, &lt;a href="https://x.com/RepLuna/status/2072651969924120971"&gt;said that now the House must attach SAVE to everything&lt;/a&gt;. Although why this would force any given action on the Senate is unclear especially when she also keeps voting against procedural rules that would advance these bills in the House after her demands are met.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The House and Senate return on July 13 and so will our previews and recaps.&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>Legislative Recap</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>All House, All Week (Maybe)</title>
      <author>Amy West       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/609/2026-06-29_all-house-all-week-maybe</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/609/2026-06-29_all-house-all-week-maybe</guid>
      <pubDate>Mon, 29 Jun 2026 15:07:16 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;p&gt;The Senate is out this week, but the House is in. There are a number of &lt;a href="https://www.congress.gov/committee-schedule/weekly/2026/06/29"&gt;committee meetings&lt;/a&gt; with &lt;a href="https://www.congress.gov/event/119th-congress/house-event/118853?s=1&amp;amp;r=1"&gt;Monday’s (6/29) Rules Committee&lt;/a&gt; meeting likely being the most consequential.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Rules will review the &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr8800"&gt;H.R. 8800: National Defense Authorization Act for Fiscal Year 2027&lt;/a&gt; and its &lt;a href="https://rules.house.gov/bill/119/hr-8800?utm_source=Sailthru&amp;amp;utm_medium=email&amp;amp;utm_campaign=6/29/26%20AM:&amp;amp;utm_term=Punchbowl%20AM%20and%20Active%20Subscribers%20from%20Memberful%20Combined"&gt;1,363 amendments&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;a href="https://punchbowl.news/article/house/gop-chaos/"&gt;As reported by Politico&lt;/a&gt;, two of those amendments are from &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/members/anna_luna/456891"&gt;Rep. Luna (R-FL13)&lt;/a&gt;, who shut down the House Floor last week when she demanded the &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s1383"&gt;SAVE America Act&lt;/a&gt; be attached to another bill like the NDAA. Politico goes on to say that Luna has said she’ll shut down the floor again if her amendments are ruled out of order and don’t get a vote.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Other Possible Votes&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.majorityleader.gov/schedule/weekly-schedule.htm"&gt;House Weekly Schedule&lt;/a&gt; lists a smaller than usual number of bills that may or may not get votes. As usual, most are coming to the floor under suspension of the rules and will need a 2/3rds majority to pass. Whether the House gets to these votes probably depends on what happens in the Rules Committee as described above. All of the bills, should they pass, would still need to get through the Senate, so no new laws are likely this week.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;KIDS Act&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;That said, of the bills on the suspension list, a particularly consequential one is called &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr7757"&gt;H.R. 7757: KIDS Act&lt;/a&gt;. This bill would decrease all users’ privacy online and lead to online speech being curtailed in the name of protecting children. Per &lt;a href="https://rollcall.com/2026/06/29/kids-online-safety-push-clouded-by-house-senate-divide/"&gt;Roll Call&lt;/a&gt;, most of the Congressional conflict is about whether it does those things &lt;em&gt;enough&lt;/em&gt;. From the perspective of privacy advocates, any version of this legislation does both too much. &lt;a href="https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2026/06/kids-act-would-require-age-checks-get-online"&gt;The Electronic Frontier Foundation&lt;/a&gt; provides an explanation of how the KIDS Act, even without the stronger Senate provisions, would do so.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Presentment Ahoy&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Per &lt;a href="https://x.com/CraigCaplan/status/2071578546636501091"&gt;Craig Caplan on X&lt;/a&gt;, Speaker Johnson said he would present the recently passed housing bill to the President today. If he does indeed do this (no indication in Congress.gov so far), then the bill would become law without the President’s signature on either July 9 or July 10, depending on whether today, 6/29, is considered day one or day zero.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;We’ll see you with a recap of whatever happens this week whenever it happens.&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <category>Legislative Preview</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Tantrums and Nearly Unanimous Votes</title>
      <author>Amy West       </author>
      <link>https://www.govtrack.us/posts/608/2026-06-26_tantrums-and-nearly-unanimous-votes</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.govtrack.us/posts/608/2026-06-26_tantrums-and-nearly-unanimous-votes</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 26 Jun 2026 14:47:20 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>                &lt;h2&gt;The President Has a Tantrum&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;The week started straightforwardly enough and then it got weird.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;On Monday, the Senate passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr6644"&gt;H.R. 6644: 21st Century ROAD to Housing Act&lt;/a&gt;. The House was expected to quickly pass it too and it did: &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h224"&gt;358-32&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;em&gt;Then&lt;/em&gt; there was going to be a big signing ceremony at the Capitol and everyone would get good press and finish up work before the July 4th break.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;But the President was mad at the Senate, so it all ground to a halt. Why was he mad? &lt;a href="https://abc7news.com/story/house-gives-final-approval-bipartisan-housing-bill-aimed-lowering-costs/19370693/?userab=kabc_content_recs-577*variant_c_trending-2482,wls_content_recs-584*variant_a_control_wls-2516,wtvd_content_recs-585*variant_a_control_wtvd-2518,ktrk_content_recs-587*variant_a_control_ktrk-2522,wpvi_content_recs-586*variant_a_control_wpvi-2520,otv_web_content_rec-539*variant_c_trending-2268,otv_web_topic-580*variant_b_topic_redesign-2509,otv_search_page_design_unification-546*variant_a_control-2299,kgo_content_recs-583*variant_a_control_kgo-2514,abcn_popular_reads_exp-542*variant_b_7days_filter-2288"&gt;He said&lt;/a&gt; it was because &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s184"&gt;the Senate agreed to a non-binding resolution already passed in the House against the war on Iran&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;So, Senate Republicans held &lt;em&gt;another&lt;/em&gt; vote on the war - on a resolution that, were it to also pass the House would have legal force - and &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/s192"&gt;voted this one down&lt;/a&gt;. Will such a transparent attempt to mollify the President work? Who knows.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Meanwhile, the housing bill is fine, just delayed from becoming law. President Trump &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/posts/592/2026-04-15_the-presidents-save-act-threat-was-empty-like-we-said"&gt;fully backed down from the same threat earlier this year&lt;/a&gt; so there’s every reason to expect he will again this time.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;Laws do not have to be signed by the President to go into effect. Once they’re presented to the President, there’s approximately 10 days for action. If no action is taken (like vetoing or signing), then the bill becomes law. Congress can hold bills until they think the time is right with no time limit on their end except for the ending of this Congress in early January 2027.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;In addition to being mad about the Iran vote, &lt;a href="https://www.politico.com/news/2026/06/24/hes-getting-pissed-trump-cancels-housing-bill-signing-over-election-bill-frustrations-00974648"&gt;the President again demanded&lt;/a&gt; that Congress pass the &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s1383"&gt;SAVE America Act&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;For this to happen, either Democratic Senators would have to vote for it or Senate Republicans would have to agree to get rid of the filibuster (the reason why Democratic Senators would have to agree to it). Democratic Senators are not going to vote for this bill which leaves killing the filibuster. President Trump’s approach is mystifying since most people respond poorly to constantly being badgered and having their work undermined, but he seems convinced that he’ll wear them down. Maybe he’s right.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Eager to get in on the fun, &lt;a href="https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2026/06/26/congress/gop-agenda-trump-tenuous-00977716?utm_source=dlvr.it&amp;amp;utm_medium=bluesky"&gt;a small group of Republicans shut down work on the House Floor on Thursday&lt;/a&gt; while demanding the Senate pass the SAVE America Act.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Despite his own blockade style tactics just one day before, President Trump has reportedly asked this small group of House Republicans to stop. Per &lt;a href="https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2026/06/26/congress/gop-agenda-trump-tenuous-00977716?utm_source=dlvr.it&amp;amp;utm_medium=bluesky"&gt;Politico&lt;/a&gt;, they agreed to stop this action, but also said they were demanding it be attached to other bills normally likely to pass. Doing so would just kill those bills in the Senate.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The bottom line is this: the SAVE America Act does not have the votes to pass both chambers of Congress. The various punitive paths described above do not appear to be winning any new votes.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;One New Law*&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;*Well, you know, eventually. Whenever it’s presented to the President and enough time passes or he agrees to sign it.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/s629"&gt;S. 629: Emergency Conservation Program Improvement Act of 2025&lt;/a&gt;, which would increase advance payments for and extend the period to use emergency conservation funds. More information here from the &lt;a href="https://forestlandowners.com/emergency-conservation-program-improvement-act/"&gt;Forest Landowners Association&lt;/a&gt;. It passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h223"&gt;368-19&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Other House Votes&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;These bills are not close to becoming law. They’ll have to get through the Senate first. Given all the drama we described at the top of the post, it’s notable that the votes the House did take were all lopsided majorities and unanimous in one case.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr7401"&gt;H.R. 7401: Small Business Lending Fraud Prevention Act &lt;/a&gt;, which would require Small Business Administration employees who approve loans to attest that they have no conflicts of interest (&lt;a href="https://www.cbo.gov/publication/62337"&gt;CBO&lt;/a&gt;), passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h225"&gt;415-0&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr915"&gt;H.R. 915: Small Business Technological Act of 2025&lt;/a&gt;, which would authorize small business loans to finance access to modern business software, passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h226"&gt;414-4&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/119/hr2478"&gt;H.R. 2478: Financial Exploitation Prevention Act of 2025&lt;/a&gt;, which would attempt to limit financial exploitation of the elderly (see &lt;a href="https://www.cbo.gov/publication/62311"&gt;CBO&lt;/a&gt; for more detail), passed &lt;a href="https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/119-2026/h227"&gt;414-2&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2026/06/26/congress/gop-agenda-trump-tenuous-00977716?utm_source=dlvr.it&amp;amp;utm_medium=bluesky"&gt;According to Politico&lt;/a&gt;, the House will be back for at least one day next week to work on bills that didn’t get votes this week. So we may have a recap for you next week covering some more substantive legislation like appropriations bills.&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
